From 63fc76f64dd852b3e6bb5fdf5459e0d6a52734b6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stephano Cetola <stephano@cetola.net>
Date: Wed, 8 Jul 2026 17:30:00 -0800
Subject: spi: rockchip: restore bus_num alias lookup via dev->id
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

ctlr->bus_num = pdev->id was commented out when this driver was
ported from Linux (pdev doesn't exist in barebox's device model here,
only dev - the port left this line unresolved rather than translating
it). With bus_num left at its kzalloc default of 0,
spi_register_controller() only runs the alias lookup when bus_num < 0,
so it's skipped and every Rockchip SPI controller ends up on bus 0.

On RK3588S, spi@feb20000 (the rk806 PMIC SPI, aliased as spi2) is
enabled and probed before the gpio-spi master. With both stealing
bus 0, spi_get_controller(0) returns the PMIC controller instead of
the intended gpio-spi RP2040 bus. The barebox spi command then does
a write+read through the Rockchip SPI driver, which programmes the
hardware in XFM_RO (RX-only) mode for the read phase, in that mode
dummy TXDR writes don't generate clock pulses, the RX FIFO never
fills, and rockchip_spi_pio's while(1) loop never exits → boot hang.

Fix: set ctlr->bus_num = dev->id, the direct translation of the
original Linux line (dev is what pdev became during the port) and
the convention most other barebox SPI drivers already use
(gpio_spi.c, mvebu_spi.c, mxs_spi.c, omap3_spi.c, spi-fsl-qspi.c,
etc). drivers/of/platform.c sets dev->id = DEVICE_ID_SINGLE (-1) for
every DT-instantiated device, and this driver is only ever
DT-instantiated, so dev->id is always -1 here - identical in effect
to hardcoding -1 directly (the convention 4 other barebox SPI drivers
use instead: stm32_spi.c, spi-sifive.c, atmel-quadspi.c,
spi-stm32-qspi.c). Either spelling triggers the same alias-lookup
path in spi_register_controller(), so this isn't a narrow
board-specific workaround: every Rockchip SPI controller across every
board using this driver is DT-instantiated, so the fix is correct
universally, not just for the Radxa CM5 board that exposed it by
being the first to actually have two Rockchip SPI controllers active
at once.

The sibling commented-out line (ctlr->mode_bits = ...) is left alone:
it's commented out in several other barebox SPI drivers too
(spi-bcm2835.c, spi-fsl-qspi.c, spi-nxp-fspi.c, spi-stm32-qspi.c) as
an established convention, and mode_bits is never read anywhere in
barebox's SPI core - it's a dead field regardless.

Signed-off-by: Stephano Cetola <stephano@cetola.net>
---
 drivers/spi/spi-rockchip.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/spi/spi-rockchip.c b/drivers/spi/spi-rockchip.c
index 1e81e9393f..b9f43fa4d6 100644
--- a/drivers/spi/spi-rockchip.c
+++ b/drivers/spi/spi-rockchip.c
@@ -514,7 +514,7 @@ static int rockchip_spi_probe(struct device *dev)
 		goto err_put_ctlr;
 	}
 
-//	ctlr->bus_num = pdev->id;
+	ctlr->bus_num = dev->id;
 //	ctlr->mode_bits = SPI_CPOL | SPI_CPHA | SPI_LOOP | SPI_LSB_FIRST;
 
 	/*
